B.vY=new A.bP(B.OT,["Total value of all a company's shares = price x shares. Bigger = larger, usually more stable company.","Price-to-Earnings. How many years of current profit you pay for the stock. Lower can mean cheaper (but check why).","Price-to-Book. Price vs the company's net assets. Under 1 can signal undervaluation; high for asset-light firms.","Return on Equity. Profit generated per rupee/dollar of shareholder money. Higher = more efficient. 15%+ is good.","How much debt vs owners' capital. Lower is safer. Above 2 can be risky.","Profit kept from each unit of sales. Higher = more profitable business.","Annual dividend as a % of price - the cash return you get just for holding.","Net assets per share (what's left for shareholders if assets were sold and debts paid).",'Benjamin Graham\'s conservative "fair value" floor from earnings and book value.',"Fair value = EPS x a fair industry P/E multiple.","Fair value = book value x a fair price-to-book (from ROE).","Discounted Cash Flow - projects future earnings and discounts them to today's value.","Values the stock purely on its future dividends.","Value if profits never grew - a no-growth baseline.","Growth-adjusted P/E: faster growers deserve a higher multiple.","Values the whole business (incl. debt) on operating profit.","Discounts 10 years of earnings with growth fading over time - good for maturing companies."],t.li)
